faq:
  - q: "What is the difference between a resume objective and a professional summary?"
    a: "A resume objective states what you want — 'seeking a role where I can apply my skills.' A professional summary states what you offer — 'Senior software engineer with 8 years building distributed systems at scale.' Objectives are candidate-centric. Summaries are hiring-manager-centric. For anyone with more than 2 years of experience, a professional summary is the right choice."
  - q: "Should I use a resume objective or summary?"
    a: "Professional summary — almost always. Resume objectives are appropriate only for entry-level candidates with no relevant experience or career changers who need to explicitly bridge their background. For mid-level to senior professionals, an objective reads as weak and self-focused. A summary reads as confident and employer-relevant."
  - q: "How do you write a strong professional summary?"
    a: "Three elements: (1) Level and role identity: 'Senior product manager' / 'Staff software engineer' / 'Director of Finance.' (2) Domain and specialization: what specifically you do or have done. (3) Value signal or targeting statement: what you produce or are optimizing for. Keep it to 2–4 sentences. Do not use adjectives like 'results-driven' or 'passionate.'"
  - q: "How long should a resume summary be?"
    a: "2–4 sentences or 3–5 bullet points. Long enough to establish identity and value, short enough to be read in the 6–10 seconds a hiring manager spends on initial review."
  - q: "Do you need a summary on your resume at all?"
    a: "Yes — for mid-to-senior professionals. The summary is the first thing a hiring manager reads. It sets the frame for how they read the rest of the resume. Without a summary, you are asking the reader to construct your identity from scattered experience sections. Do not make them do that work."
