<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow section-title-center">Objective vs. summary — what each one actually says</h2>
    <div class="two-col-grid" style="margin-top:32px;">
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>Resume objective — what it is</h3>
        <p>A resume objective states what the candidate is looking for. It is candidate-centric. The implicit message is: "Here is what I want from you." For hiring managers reviewing dozens of resumes, an objective section consumes space that could signal value and instead signals that the candidate is thinking about their own needs first.</p>
        <div style="padding:12px 16px; margin-top:16px; background:rgba(255,80,80,0.06); border-radius:8px; border-left:3px solid rgba(255,80,80,0.3);">
          <strong style="font-size:0.85rem;">Typical objective (do not use this):</strong>
          <p style="margin:6px 0 0; font-size:0.9rem;">"Seeking a challenging software engineering role where I can apply my 6 years of experience and continue to grow as a developer."</p>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:16px; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--muted);">This tells the hiring manager nothing about your capability, your level, or your fit. It tells them what you want. They do not need to know what you want at this stage — they need to know what you offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>Professional summary — what it is</h3>
        <p>A professional summary states what the candidate offers. It is hiring-manager-centric. The implicit message is: "Here is what you get when you hire me." It establishes identity, seniority, domain, and value in 2–4 sentences — setting the frame for how the reader interprets the rest of the resume.</p>
        <div style="padding:12px 16px; margin-top:16px; background:rgba(87,207,196,0.06); border-radius:8px; border-left:3px solid rgba(87,207,196,0.3);">
          <strong style="font-size:0.85rem;">Strong summary (use this instead):</strong>
          <p style="margin:6px 0 0; font-size:0.9rem;">"Senior software engineer with 6 years of experience building distributed backend systems at Series B and Series C companies. Specializing in event-driven architecture, high-throughput APIs, and platform reliability. Currently targeting staff-level engineering roles at product-led B2B SaaS companies."</p>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:16px; font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--muted);">In three sentences: seniority, domain, specialization, and targeting signal. The reader knows exactly who this candidate is before reading a single bullet.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow section-title-center">The professional summary formula — three elements</h2>
    <div class="two-col-grid" style="margin-top:32px;">
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>Element 1: Level and role identity</h3>
        <p>Open with your level and your role title — the same title (or close match) that appears in the job description you are targeting. This is the single most important signal in your summary. It tells the hiring manager your altitude immediately.</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>"Senior product manager..." — not "Product professional with experience in..."</li>
          <li>"Director of Finance..." — not "Finance leader with a track record of..."</li>
          <li>"Staff software engineer..." — not "Experienced software engineer..."</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style="margin-top:24px;">Element 2: Domain and specialization</h3>
        <p>Name what you specifically do within your function. Not the generic version — the specific one. This is where keyword matching happens and where you differentiate from other candidates at the same level.</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>"...specializing in cloud-native data infrastructure and streaming pipelines"</li>
          <li>"...with a focus on enterprise B2B go-to-market strategy and revenue operations"</li>
          <li>"...background in credit risk modeling and regulatory capital management"</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>Element 3: Value signal or targeting statement</h3>
        <p>Close with what you produce or where you are going. This is optional but strong — it signals a focused professional with a clear direction, not someone applying to everything.</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>"Targeting engineering manager or staff IC roles at Series B–D companies building infrastructure products."</li>
          <li>"Track record of reducing time-to-insight by 40%+ through platform modernization and data governance initiatives."</li>
          <li>"Now seeking a Chief of Staff or VP Strategy role at a pre-IPO company scaling from $50M to $200M ARR."</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style="margin-top:24px;">What to exclude from your summary</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Adjectives without evidence: "results-driven," "passionate," "dynamic," "strategic thinker"</li>
          <li>Generic phrases: "excellent communication skills," "team player," "proven track record"</li>
          <li>Anything about what you are looking for — that is an objective, not a summary</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

